## Strict Rules for Noise Filtering (The "Signals" Flags)
If you don't filter, a "soft 404" page will cause ffuf to return tens of thousands of fake results. Master these rules to isolate valid targets: [3] 

| Filter Flag | Rule / Logic | Usage Example |
|---|---|---|
| -fc | Filter Codes: Hides responses matching specific HTTP status codes. | -fc 403,404 (ignores forbidden/not found) |
| -fs | Filter Size: Hides responses with an exact byte size. | -fs 2341 (ignores standard error templates) |
| -fw | Filter Words: Hides responses matching an exact word-count length. | -fw 12 |
| -fl | Filter Lines: Hides responses matching an exact number of lines. | -fl 45 |

------------------------------
## Bug Bounty Safety & Operational Rules

   1. The Concurrency Rule (-t): By default, ffuf spawns 40 threads. On fragile production servers, running uncontrolled multi-threading can cause a Denial of Service (DoS), violating your bounty program rules. Control it using -t.
   * Safe scan: -t 20
      * Fast/Aggressive scan: -t 100 [6, 10] 
   2. The Rate Limiting Rule (-p / -rate): To avoid getting your IP banned by Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) like Cloudflare, inject artificial delays.
   * -p 0.1: Pauses 0.1 seconds between requests.
      * -rate 50: Restricts the engine to a max of 50 requests per second. [8, 11] 
   3. The Evidence Rule (-o): Always save your output to document your findings. Program platforms accept structured JSON reports.
   * -o results.json -of json [3, 8] 
   
